// Authored content for 3.1.10.2 Biological classification (OxfordAQA 9610 v5.1, printed page 19; statements
// 3.1.10.2.s1–s3 in data/spec.js). Batch 9, owned by the 3.1.10 author.
// Scope: classification arranges species in groups that reflect relationships and may reflect evolutionary origins; a
// hierarchy of nested groups, each a taxon; one hierarchy is domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus,
// species; and, as a skill, using GIVEN data to show how immunology and genome sequencing help clarify relationships.
// Domain names (Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya), antibody structure (3.2.5.2) and the history of particular revisions are
// supporting only. Classifications of named organisms come from the model in _species-art.js; every difference count
// quoted for the imaginary species P, Q, R, S is computed from the sequences in that model.
window.BIO = window.BIO || {}; window.BIO.content = window.BIO.content || {};
(function () {
  const A = window.BIO.speciesArt, M = window.BIO.speciesModel;
  const R = "3.1.10.2";
  const S = (...n) => n.map((k) => `${R}.s${k}`);
  const ILL = '<span class="badge rose">illustrative data</span>';
  const mx = M.matrix();
  const W = M.WEIGH;
  const tbl = (rows, head) => `<table class="tbl"><thead><tr>${head.map((h) => `<th>${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map((r) => `<tr>${r.map((c) => `<td>${c}</td>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</tbody></table>`;
  const orgRow = (k) => [M.ORGS[k].common, ...M.ORGS[k].taxa.map((t, i) => (i >= 6 ? `<em>${t}</em>` : t))];
  const weighTables = () => `<div class="grid grid-2"><div><p class="small"><strong>Immunological data.</strong> Precipitate as % of that with the reference species' own serum.</p>${tbl([["W", W.antiW[0][1], W.antiX[1][1]], ["X", W.antiW[1][1], W.antiX[0][1]], ["Y", W.antiW[2][1], W.antiX[2][1]], ["Z", W.antiW[3][1], W.antiX[3][1]]], ["Serum from", "Antiserum against W albumin", "Antiserum against X albumin"])}</div><div><p class="small"><strong>Sequence data.</strong> Number of base differences in ${String(W.bases).replace(/\B(?=(\d{3})+(?!\d))/g, " ")} aligned bases from 12 genes.</p>${tbl([["W", "–", W.seqDiff["W-X"], W.seqDiff["W-Y"], W.seqDiff["W-Z"]], ["X", "", "–", W.seqDiff["X-Y"], W.seqDiff["X-Z"]], ["Y", "", "", "–", W.seqDiff["Y-Z"]]], ["", "W", "X", "Y", "Z"])}</div></div>`;

  window.BIO.content[R] = {
    status: "draft",
    banner: "museum-specimens", bannerText: "Drawers of specimens, and now databases of genome sequences: classification is a continuing effort to arrange the living world so that the groups reflect real relationships.",
    keywords: ["classification", "taxonomy", "taxon", "taxa", "hierarchy", "domain", "kingdom", "phylum", "class", "order", "family", "genus", "species", "evolutionary relationships", "common ancestor", "phylogeny", "immunology", "immunological comparison", "antibodies", "antiserum", "precipitate", "albumin", "genome sequencing", "base sequence", "DNA comparison", "Eukarya", "Bacteria", "Archaea", "Hominidae"],
    notes: () => `
<div class="notice"><strong>What the specification requires (3.1.10.2).</strong> (1) Biological classification attempts to arrange species in groups that <strong>reflect their relationships</strong> and <strong>may reflect their evolutionary origins</strong>. It uses a <strong>hierarchy</strong> in which smaller groups are placed within larger groups; each group is called a <strong>taxon</strong>. (2) One taxonomic hierarchy comprises the taxa <strong>domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species</strong>. (3) You should be able to <strong>use given data</strong> to show how <strong>immunology</strong> and <strong>genome sequencing</strong> help to clarify taxonomic relationships. The names of the three domains, details of antibodies (3.2.5) and the history of particular revisions are supporting only. <span class="badge teal">required</span> <span class="badge">supporting</span> <span class="badge amber">extension</span></div>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s1">1. What classification tries to do <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p><strong>Classification</strong> (taxonomy) is the arrangement of species into groups. Biological classification <strong>attempts to arrange species in groups that reflect their relationships</strong>: species placed in the same group are more alike, and more closely related, than species placed in different groups.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Relationships</strong> are judged from shared features: body structure, development, biochemistry, and now the base sequences of DNA and the amino acid sequences of proteins.</li>
<li>Because all living things share common ancestors, close relationship means a <strong>recent common ancestor</strong>. A classification that groups species by their descent from common ancestors <strong>reflects their evolutionary origins</strong>.</li>
<li>The specification says classification <em>may</em> reflect evolutionary origins. Linnaeus grouped species by visible similarities a century before Darwin's theory of evolution. Modern classifications aim to reflect evolutionary history, but some traditional groupings do not, and classifications are revised as new evidence (especially molecular evidence) appears.</li></ul>
<p class="small"><span class="badge amber">extension</span> Example: the traditional class Reptilia excludes birds, yet birds are descended from one group of reptiles (dinosaurs). A group defined by evolutionary origins would include all the descendants of a common ancestor, so in evolutionary terms "reptiles" without birds is not a complete group. Many textbook classifications keep the class Aves for convenience.</p>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s1 ${R}.s2">2. A hierarchy of taxa <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Classification uses a <strong>hierarchy</strong>: <strong>smaller groups are placed within larger groups</strong>, and there is <strong>no overlap</strong> between groups at the same level. A genus lies entirely within one family; a family entirely within one order. Each group, at any level, is called a <strong>taxon</strong> (plural <strong>taxa</strong>): Felidae is a taxon at the rank of family, and Mammalia is a taxon at the rank of class.</p>
<div class="notice sage"><strong>One taxonomic hierarchy</strong> (largest to smallest): <strong>domain → kingdom → phylum → class → order → family → genus → species</strong>.<br><span class="small">A memory aid: <em>Do Keep Ponds Clean Or Frogs Get Sick</em>.</span></div>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.nested("lion")}</div></div>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Domain</th><th>Kingdom</th><th>Phylum</th><th>Class</th><th>Order</th><th>Family</th><th>Genus</th><th>Species</th></tr></thead><tbody>
${["human", "chimp", "cat", "lion", "sparrow", "palm"].map((k) => `<tr>${orgRow(k).map((c) => `<td>${c}</td>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</tbody></table>
<p class="small muted">${M.ORGS.palm.note}</p>
<ul><li>Moving <strong>down</strong> the hierarchy, each taxon contains <strong>fewer species</strong>, and those species share <strong>more features</strong> and (in a classification that reflects evolution) a <strong>more recent common ancestor</strong>.</li>
<li>Taxon names above genus begin with a capital letter and are not normally written in italics (Mammalia, Felidae). Genus and species names are written in italics (3.1.10.1).</li>
<li>The specification says <em>one</em> taxonomic hierarchy: biologists also use extra ranks (subfamily, tribe, subspecies and others), and different systems place some groups differently.</li></ul>
<p class="small"><span class="badge">supporting</span> <strong>The three domains.</strong> Living organisms are divided into three domains: <strong>Bacteria</strong>, <strong>Archaea</strong> (prokaryotes that differ from bacteria in, for example, their membrane lipids and RNA polymerase) and <strong>Eukarya</strong> (all eukaryotes: animals, plants, fungi and protoctists). The three-domain system, proposed by Carl Woese and colleagues in 1990, was based on comparing the base sequences of ribosomal RNA. You need to know that domain is the highest taxon, not the domain names.</p>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s1 ${R}.s2">3. Reading a classification: what sharing a taxon tells you <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.tree()}</div></div>
<p>The lowest taxon two species share is a measure of how closely they are classified. The lion and tiger share a genus (<em>Panthera</em>); the lion and domestic cat share only a family (Felidae); the lion and human share a class (Mammalia); the lion and date palm share only a domain.</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Sharing a lower taxon <strong>does</strong> suggest</th><th>It does <strong>not</strong> tell you</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>More features in common</td><td>That one species evolved from the other (lions did not evolve from tigers, nor humans from chimpanzees)</td></tr>
<tr><td>If the classification reflects evolution: a more recent common ancestor than with species that share only a higher taxon</td><td>How long ago the common ancestor lived</td></tr>
<tr><td>A greater similarity of DNA and proteins is expected</td><td>Which of several species in the same taxon is the closest relative: humans, chimpanzees and gorillas are all in the family Hominidae, so the classification alone cannot say which ape is closest to us</td></tr>
<tr><td></td><td>That taxa of the same rank in different groups are equivalent: a family of beetles and a family of mammals need not be the same age or equally varied</td></tr></tbody></table>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s3">4. Evidence from immunology <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Proteins from closely related species have similar amino acid sequences and therefore similar shapes. <strong>Antibodies</strong> bind only to molecules of a particular shape, so they can be used to compare a protein, such as serum albumin from the blood, between species.</p>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.immuno()}</div></div>
<ol><li>The protein (for example serum albumin) from the reference species, A, is injected into an animal such as a rabbit, which makes <strong>antibodies</strong> against it. Serum containing these antibodies (an <strong>antiserum</strong>) is collected.</li>
<li>The antiserum is mixed with serum from each species to be compared, including A itself.</li>
<li>The antibodies bind to the protein where its shape matches, forming complexes that come out of solution as a <strong>precipitate</strong>.</li>
<li>The <strong>more precipitate</strong>, the <strong>more similar the protein</strong> to that of species A, so the <strong>more closely related</strong> the species is to A (the more recent their common ancestor). Results are often given as a percentage of the precipitate formed with A's own serum.</li></ol>
<p class="small"><span class="badge">supporting</span> The antibodies bind to particular regions (antigenic sites) on the protein surface. Antibodies and antigen–antibody complexes are explained in 3.2.5.2. In 1904 George Nuttall used precipitin tests on the blood of hundreds of animals and found that antiserum against human serum reacted most strongly with ape serum, less with monkey serum and only weakly with that of other mammals.</p>
<div class="notice"><strong>Using given data</strong> ${ILL} Antiserum was made against albumin from species ${M.IMMUNO.antiserum} and tested with serum from five species.<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.bars(M.IMMUNO.values, { title: "Antiserum against albumin of species P" })}</div></div>
<em>Interpretation.</em> P gives the most precipitate (100 %) because the antibodies were made against its own albumin. Q gives the most of the others (86 %), so its albumin is most similar to P's: Q is most closely related to P, then S (64 %), then R (42 %); T (12 %) is least closely related. Note what the data <strong>cannot</strong> show: they compare each species only with P, so they do not show whether Q is more closely related to S or to R. That needs antisera against other species' albumin.</div>
<h3>Limitations of immunological evidence</h3>
<ul><li>It compares <strong>one protein</strong> (or a few), a tiny part of the genome; different proteins can give somewhat different answers.</li>
<li>Antibodies detect <strong>shape at particular sites</strong>, not the whole amino acid sequence: a change at an antigenic site can reduce binding a lot, while several changes elsewhere may have little effect.</li>
<li>The amount of precipitate is only <strong>semi-quantitative</strong> and depends on the antiserum (each animal's antibodies differ), the concentrations and the conditions; repeats and controls are needed, and small differences may not be meaningful.</li>
<li>Results need not be the same in both directions (antiserum against A tested with B's serum, and antiserum against B tested with A's).</li></ul>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s3">5. Evidence from genome sequencing <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>The base sequence of DNA can be determined for genes or for whole genomes. Over time, mutations change the base sequence in each lineage separately, so the longer ago two species shared a common ancestor, the more differences their DNA is likely to have accumulated. Comparing <strong>aligned sequences</strong> (lined up so that equivalent bases are compared) and counting the differences therefore shows how closely species are related: <strong>fewer differences, more closely related, more recent common ancestor</strong>.</p>
<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.seqCompare("P")}</div></div>
<p>In these illustrative sequences P and Q differ at ${mx.P.Q} positions, P and S at ${mx.P.S} and P and R at ${mx.P.R}. P and Q are most closely related; S is next; R is the most distant of the four. The sequence data agree with the immunological data for P, Q, S and R above. Unlike the immunological data, a matrix of differences compares <em>every</em> pair, so it also shows that Q is as closely related to S (${mx.Q.S}) as P is.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Strengths.</strong> Sequences give a very large amount of exact, countable data. They include changes that do not alter the protein (because the genetic code is degenerate, 3.1.8.1, and much DNA is non-coding), so they detect differences that immunology misses. Comparing many genes, or whole genomes, reduces the effect of any one unusual gene.</li>
<li><strong>Limitations.</strong> Different genes can suggest slightly different relationships, so many genes are needed. Genes and lineages change at different rates, so the number of differences is not a simple clock. As differences build up, a base can change more than once at the same position, so counts underestimate the real number of changes between distantly related species. Hybridisation can carry DNA between species (as in polar and brown bears). DNA from old remains is usually degraded.</li></ul>
<p class="small"><span class="badge">supporting</span> <strong>What sequencing has changed.</strong> (1) Humans and chimpanzees: when the chimpanzee genome was published in 2005, about 1.2 % of the aligned single bases differed from the human genome (more if insertions and deletions are counted). Genome comparisons show that chimpanzees and bonobos are our closest living relatives, closer to us than to gorillas or orangutans. Older classifications put the great apes in a family (Pongidae) separate from humans (Hominidae); because that grouping does not reflect evolutionary relationships, humans and great apes are now placed together in the Hominidae. (2) Whales: DNA evidence in the 1990s showed that the closest living relatives of whales are hippopotamuses, and fossils of early whales with ankle bones like those of hippos and their relatives, described in 2001, supported this.</p>

<h2 data-stmt="${R}.s3 ${R}.s1">6. Weighing the evidence <span class="badge teal">required</span></h2>
<p>Different kinds of evidence usually agree. When they do not, consider <strong>how much data</strong> each is based on, <strong>how precise</strong> it is, and whether a difference is <strong>large enough to be meaningful</strong>.</p>
<div class="notice">${ILL} Four imaginary species of desert gecko, W, X, Y and Z, were compared.${weighTables()}
<em>Evaluation.</em> With antiserum against W, Y gives slightly more precipitate (${W.antiW[2][1]} %) than X (${W.antiW[1][1]} %), suggesting Y is the closer relative of W. But the difference is small, the method is semi-quantitative, and it rests on one protein. With antiserum against X, W reacts more (${W.antiX[1][1]} %) than Y (${W.antiX[2][1]} %). The sequence data are based on ${String(W.bases).replace(/\B(?=(\d{3})+(?!\d))/g, " ")} bases from 12 genes and show W and X differing at only ${W.seqDiff["W-X"]} bases, compared with ${W.seqDiff["W-Y"]} for W and Y. The stronger conclusion is that <strong>W and X are most closely related</strong>, with Y next and Z the most distant. Repeating the immunological tests, and using more proteins, would show whether the small W–Y result is reliable.</div>
<div class="notice amber"><strong>Common traps.</strong> "More precipitate means less related" (the reverse is true). "The species with the most precipitate is the closest relative" when that species is the reference species itself. Concluding a close relationship between two test species from their results against a third species' antiserum. Treating a difference of a few percent as decisive.</div>`,
    diagrams: [
      { id: "d-3.1.10.2-1", title: "Nested taxa: the classification of the lion", status: "reviewed", caption: "Each taxon sits entirely within the one above it, from domain Eukarya to the species Panthera leo. The grey notes name other organisms in each taxon.", alt: "Eight nested boxes. Domain Eukarya, also plants, fungi and protoctists; kingdom Animalia, also insects, worms, fish; phylum Chordata, also fish, amphibians, birds; class Mammalia, also humans, bats, whales; order Carnivora, also dogs, bears, seals; family Felidae, also the domestic cat; genus Panthera, also the tiger and leopard; species Panthera leo.", svg: A.nested("lion") },
      { id: "d-3.1.10.2-2", title: "Classification tree for seven organisms", status: "reviewed", caption: "One row per rank. The lower two organisms join, the more taxa they share. Lion and tiger share a genus; the domestic cat joins them at family; humans and chimpanzees join at family Hominidae; all the mammals join at class; the house sparrow joins at phylum; the date palm only at domain. The genus is abbreviated in the species row.", alt: "A tree with rows labelled domain to species. Eukarya splits into Animalia and Plantae. Animalia leads to Chordata, which splits into Mammalia and Aves. Mammalia splits into Primates and Carnivora. Primates leads to Hominidae, splitting into Homo and Pan (H. sapiens, P. troglodytes). Carnivora leads to Felidae, splitting into Felis (F. catus) and Panthera (P. leo, P. tigris). Aves leads to Passeriformes, Passeridae, Passer, P. domesticus. Plantae leads to Angiospermophyta, Liliopsida, Arecales, Arecaceae, Phoenix, P. dactylifera.", svg: A.tree() },
      { id: "d-3.1.10.2-3", title: "Comparing a protein between species with antibodies", status: "reviewed", caption: "Albumin from species A is injected into a rabbit; the rabbit's antiserum contains antibodies against A's albumin; the antiserum is mixed with serum from each species. More precipitate means a protein more like A's, so a closer relationship. Schematic.", alt: "Step 1, a tube of serum albumin from species A, arrow labelled injected to a rabbit (step 2, which makes antibodies specific to A's albumin), arrow labelled blood taken to a tube of antiserum containing antibodies (step 3). Step 4, four tubes of serum from species A, B, C and D with antiserum added, with the pale precipitate layer largest in A and smallest in D. A box states that more precipitate means albumin more similar to A's, so more closely related.", svg: A.immuno() },
      { id: "d-3.1.10.2-4", title: "Comparing aligned base sequences", status: "reviewed", caption: `Illustrative sequences for imaginary species. Bases that differ from P are boxed; the matrix gives the number of differences for every pair (P–Q ${mx.P.Q}, P–S ${mx.P.S}, P–R ${mx.P.R}). Fewer differences mean a more recent common ancestor.`, alt: `Four aligned rows of 30 bases for P, Q, S and R, with differences from P boxed and counts beside each row (Q ${mx.P.Q}, S ${mx.P.S}, R ${mx.P.R}). Below, a four by four table of differences between every pair.`, svg: A.seqCompare("P") },
    ],
    interactives: [
      { kind: "spcBuilder", title: "Taxonomy Builder", note: "First build the hierarchy of ranks for the lion, from the largest taxon to the smallest, by clicking (or pressing Enter on) the ranks in order. Then place the correct taxon at each rank for at least two more organisms.",
        organisms: ["human", "sparrow", "palm", "bee"],
        items: [{ label: "Questions", questions: [
          { id: `q-${R}-tb1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which rank lies between class and family?", options: ["Order", "Phylum", "Genus", "Kingdom"], answer: 0, explanation: "Class, order, family." },
          { id: `q-${R}-tb2`, ref: R, stmts: S(2, 1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Use the builder: the lion and the house sparrow share every taxon down to and including:", options: ["the kingdom", "the phylum", "the class", "the order"], answer: 1, explanation: "Both are in phylum Chordata; the lion is in class Mammalia and the sparrow in class Aves." },
          { id: `q-${R}-tb3`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "The western honey bee is in the kingdom Animalia but the phylum Arthropoda. Which statement is correct?", options: ["The bee and the lion share a kingdom but not a phylum", "The bee and the lion share a phylum but not a kingdom", "The bee and the date palm share a kingdom", "The bee is in the same class as the house sparrow"], answer: 0, explanation: "Both are animals; the lion is a chordate and the bee an arthropod." },
        ] }] },
      { kind: "spcExplorer", title: "Classification Explorer", note: "Choose any two organisms. The table shows their taxa side by side, marks every taxon they share, and explains what the lowest shared taxon implies and what it does not.",
        items: [{ label: "Questions", questions: [
          { id: `q-${R}-ex1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Compare the human and the domestic cat. What is the lowest taxon they share?", options: ["The order Primates", "The class Mammalia", "The family Felidae", "The phylum Chordata only"], answer: 1, explanation: "Both are mammals; they are in different orders (Primates and Carnivora)." },
          { id: `q-${R}-ex2`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which pair shares a genus?", options: ["Domestic cat and lion", "Human and chimpanzee", "Lion and tiger", "Grey wolf and domestic cat"], answer: 2, explanation: "Both are Panthera. Cat and lion share only the family Felidae; human and chimpanzee only the family Hominidae." },
          { id: `q-${R}-ex3`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Lions and tigers share a genus; lions and domestic cats share a family. If the classification reflects evolutionary origins, this suggests that:", options: ["lions evolved from tigers", "lions and tigers have a more recent common ancestor than lions and domestic cats", "domestic cats are older than lions", "lions and tigers can always interbreed to give fertile offspring"], answer: 1, explanation: "A shared lower taxon suggests a more recent common ancestor, not descent of one living species from another." },
          { id: `q-${R}-ex4`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Humans, chimpanzees and gorillas are all in the family Hominidae but in different genera. Can this classification tell you whether chimpanzees or gorillas are more closely related to humans?", options: ["Yes: chimpanzees, because Pan comes first alphabetically", "Yes: gorillas, because they are larger", "No: they share the same lowest taxon with humans, so other evidence such as genome sequences is needed", "No: humans cannot be classified"], answer: 2, explanation: "The classification places all three in one family; genome comparisons show that chimpanzees (and bonobos) are the closest living relatives of humans." },
        ] }] },
      { kind: "spcMolecular", title: "Molecular Evidence Investigator", note: "Three investigations with illustrative data for imaginary species: immunological data, sequence data you can inspect base by base, and a case where the two kinds of evidence must be weighed against each other.",
        items: [
          { label: "1. Immunological data", questions: [
            { id: `q-${R}-mi1`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Using the immunological data, which species is most closely related to P?", options: ["P itself", "Q", "S", "T"], answer: 1, explanation: "Of the species tested against P's antiserum, Q gives the most precipitate (86 %). P's own 100 % is the reference, not a relative." },
            { id: `q-${R}-mi2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "sequence", marks: 2, stem: "Put the four other species in order from most to least closely related to P, using the immunological data.", steps: ["Q (86 %)", "S (64 %)", "R (42 %)", "T (12 %)"] },
            { id: `q-${R}-mi3`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "What can these data NOT show?", options: ["Which species is most closely related to P", "That T is the most distantly related to P of these species", "Whether Q is more closely related to S or to R", "That P's own serum gives the most precipitate"], answer: 2, explanation: "Every species was compared only with P. Comparing Q with S and R needs antiserum against another species' protein, or sequence data for every pair." },
          ] },
          { label: "2. Sequence data", questions: [
            { id: `q-${R}-mi4`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: "Use the alignment (highlight differences from P if you wish).", text: "Number of bases that differ between P and Q: {{1}}. Between P and S: {{2}}. Between P and R: {{3}}.", answers: { 1: [String(mx.P.Q)], 2: [String(mx.P.S)], 3: [String(mx.P.R)] }, explanation: `P–Q ${mx.P.Q}, P–S ${mx.P.S}, P–R ${mx.P.R}.` },
            { id: `q-${R}-mi5`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Do the sequence data agree with the immunological data for P, Q, S and R?", options: ["Yes: both put Q closest to P, then S, then R", "No: the sequence data put R closest to P", "No: the sequence data put S closest to P", "They cannot be compared"], answer: 0, explanation: `Fewest differences from P: Q (${mx.P.Q}), then S (${mx.P.S}), then R (${mx.P.R}); the same order as the precipitate data.` },
            { id: `q-${R}-mi6`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Highlight differences from S. What do the sequence data show that the immunological data could not?", options: ["That S is more closely related to R than to P", `That Q and P are equally different from S (${mx.Q.S} and ${mx.P.S} bases), so Q is as closely related to S as P is`, "That S is the reference species", "Nothing: sequence data only compare species with P"], answer: 1, explanation: "A difference matrix compares every pair; the immunological data compared each species with P only." },
          ] },
          { label: "3. Weigh the evidence", questions: [
            { id: `q-${R}-mi7`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "With antiserum against W albumin, Y gives 81 % precipitate and X gives 78 %. What is the best interpretation of this difference on its own?", options: ["Y is definitely the closest relative of W", "The difference is small and the method semi-quantitative, so it does not reliably separate X and Y", "X and Y are the same species", "W is more closely related to Z"], answer: 1, explanation: "Three percentage points from one protein and one antiserum is weak evidence." },
            { id: `q-${R}-mi8`, ref: R, stmts: S(3, 1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Taking all the evidence together, which conclusion is best supported?", options: ["W is most closely related to Y", "W is most closely related to X; Y is next; Z is the most distant", "W is most closely related to Z", "All four species are equally related"], answer: 1, explanation: `The sequence data (12 genes, ${W.seqDiff["W-X"]} v ${W.seqDiff["W-Y"]} v ${W.seqDiff["W-Z"]} differences) and the antiserum against X both point to W and X; only the small result with antiserum against W disagrees.` },
            { id: `q-${R}-mi9`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "short", marks: 4, stem: "The immunological data with antiserum against W suggest that Y is W's closest relative, but the sequence data suggest X. Evaluate the two kinds of evidence and state which conclusion is better supported. (4 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Immunological test compares one protein / shape at antigenic sites only; sequence data come from 12 genes / 12 000 bases, far more data", marks: 1 }, { point: "The immunological difference between X and Y is small (78 % v 81 %) and the method semi-quantitative, so may not be significant / needs repeats", marks: 1 }, { point: "The sequence difference is large (96 v 236 differences) and the antiserum against X also puts W closest to X (84 % v 72 %)", marks: 1 }, { point: "Conclusion: W is most closely related to X; further evidence such as repeated tests or more proteins would confirm", marks: 1 }], model: "The immunological evidence compares a single protein, and antibodies detect only the shape of certain sites on it; the result also depends on the particular antiserum, so the amount of precipitate is only semi-quantitative. The difference between X (78 %) and Y (81 %) is small and may not be meaningful. The sequence data are based on 12 000 bases from 12 genes and show a large difference: W and X differ at 96 bases, W and Y at 236. The antiserum against X also reacts more with W (84 %) than with Y (72 %). The better-supported conclusion is that W and X are most closely related; repeating the immunological test, or testing more proteins, would show whether the W–Y result was reliable.", commonErrors: "Accepting the immunological result because it was listed first; ignoring the size of the differences." },
          ] },
        ] },
    ],
    focus: [
      { slot: "museum-specimens", title: "Specimen collections: the reference library of classification", ref: R, explanation: () => `<p>Natural history museums and herbaria hold hundreds of millions of preserved animals and pressed plants, each labelled with where and when it was collected. When a species is named, one specimen is designated as its <strong>type specimen</strong>: the physical reference for that name, so that any biologist can check exactly which organism the name refers to. Collections let taxonomists compare the features of many individuals from across a species' range, and so decide where the boundaries between species and higher taxa lie. They have become even more valuable now that DNA can be extracted from dried skins, bones and pinned insects: specimens collected over a century ago can be sequenced and compared with living populations, or with species that have since become extinct. Classifications are hypotheses about relationships, and collections are the evidence that lets those hypotheses be tested and revised.</p>`,
        question: { id: `q-${R}-fx1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 3), type: "short", marks: 2, stem: "Suggest two ways in which large museum collections of preserved specimens help biologists to produce classifications that reflect evolutionary relationships. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Allow comparison of features (structure) of many individuals / of related species from across their range", marks: 1 }, { point: "DNA can be extracted from specimens (including old or extinct ones) and sequences compared; type specimens fix what a name refers to so classifications can be checked and revised", marks: 1 }], model: "Collections let taxonomists compare the structure of many individuals of related species from many places, so that shared features, and therefore relationships, can be judged reliably. DNA can also be extracted from preserved specimens, including those of extinct species, so that base sequences can be compared; and type specimens fix exactly which organism each name refers to, so classifications can be checked and revised when new evidence appears.", commonErrors: "Answers about public education rather than classification." } },
      { slot: "gel-sequencing", title: "Reading DNA: from gels to genomes", ref: R, explanation: () => `<p>The first practical method for reading the base sequence of DNA was published by Frederick Sanger and colleagues in 1977. DNA fragments of different lengths, each ending in a known base, were separated on a gel, and the sequence was read as a ladder of bands. Later the bases were labelled with four fluorescent dyes and read automatically as coloured peaks on a trace. Today, machines read millions of fragments at once and computers assemble them into complete genomes. The Human Genome Project published an essentially complete human sequence in 2003 (the last gaps were filled in 2022), and the chimpanzee genome followed in 2005. Comparing whole genomes, rather than a single protein, gives taxonomists billions of characters to compare, and has settled relationships, such as which apes are our closest living relatives, that older evidence left open. How sequencing works is not required for 3.1.10; interpreting the data it produces is.</p>`,
        question: { id: `q-${R}-fx2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Explain why comparing the base sequences of many genes gives more reliable evidence of relationships between species than an immunological comparison of one protein. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Many genes / far more data (bases) compared, so the effect of any one unusual gene or protein is reduced", marks: 1 }, { point: "Sequences detect every base difference, including those that do not change the amino acid (degenerate code) or are in non-coding DNA", marks: 1 }, { point: "Immunological tests detect only differences in shape at the antigenic sites of one protein and are semi-quantitative / depend on the antiserum", marks: 1 }], model: "Comparing the base sequences of many genes uses a very large amount of data, so the result does not depend on one gene that may have changed unusually fast or slowly. Sequencing detects every base difference, including silent changes that do not alter the amino acid, because the code is degenerate, and differences in non-coding DNA. An immunological test detects only differences in the shape of one protein at the sites where antibodies bind, and the amount of precipitate is only semi-quantitative, depending on the antiserum used.", commonErrors: "Saying immunology measures DNA; saying sequencing is 'more accurate' without explaining why." } },
    ],
    examFocus: `<ul><li>Learn the order exactly: <strong>domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species</strong>. A hierarchy means smaller groups within larger groups, with no overlap; each group is a <strong>taxon</strong>.</li><li>Say that classification arranges species in groups that reflect <strong>relationships</strong> and may reflect <strong>evolutionary origins</strong> (common ancestry).</li><li>Immunology: antibodies against a protein of species A; mixed with serum of other species; <strong>more precipitate = more similar protein = more closely related</strong> = more recent common ancestor. The reference species' own result is the 100 % control, not a relative.</li><li>Sequences: <strong>fewer base differences = more closely related</strong>. Quote figures from the data in your answer.</li><li>When asked to evaluate, compare how much data each method uses (one protein v many genes), its precision (semi-quantitative precipitate v exact base counts) and whether differences are large enough to matter.</li><li>Sharing a taxon does not mean one species evolved from the other.</li><li>Do not name the three domains unless asked; "domain" as a rank is what is required.</li></ul>`,
    synoptic: [
      { ref: "3.1.10.1", text: "Species, the basic unit, are grouped into the higher taxa of the hierarchy." },
      { ref: "3.1.11.1", text: "Interpreting base and amino acid sequence data to suggest relationships between organisms." },
      { ref: "3.1.8.1", text: "The degenerate genetic code: some base changes do not change the protein, so DNA comparisons detect more differences." },
      { ref: "3.1.8.3", text: "Amino acid sequence determines protein shape, which is what antibodies recognise." },
      { ref: "3.2.5.2", text: "Antibodies and the formation of antigen–antibody complexes." },
      { ref: "3.3.8.2", text: "Speciation: the evolutionary process that produces the branching pattern classification attempts to reflect." },
    ],
    quickCheck: [
      { id: `q-${R}-qc1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which list is in the correct order, from largest to smallest?", options: ["Kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus", "Kingdom, class, phylum, family, order, genus", "Phylum, kingdom, class, order, genus, family", "Kingdom, phylum, order, class, family, genus"], answer: 0, explanation: "Domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc2`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "What is a taxon?", options: ["Any group in a classification, at any rank", "Only the species", "The scientific name of an organism", "A characteristic used to identify organisms"], answer: 0, explanation: "Each group in the hierarchy is a taxon." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc3`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Antiserum against the albumin of species A gives more precipitate with serum from species B than with serum from species C. This suggests that:", options: ["C is more closely related to A than B is", "B is more closely related to A than C is", "B and C are the same species", "A is not related to B"], answer: 1, explanation: "More precipitate: more similar protein: closer relationship." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc4`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "The same gene was sequenced in four species. Which pair is most closely related?", options: ["The pair with the most base differences", "The pair with the fewest base differences", "The pair with the longest gene", "The pair with the most adenine"], answer: 1, explanation: "Fewest differences: most recent common ancestor." },
      { id: `q-${R}-qc5`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Two species are in the same family. Which must also be true?", options: ["They are in the same genus", "They are in the same order", "They can interbreed", "They have identical DNA"], answer: 1, explanation: "A family lies entirely within one order (and all the taxa above it)." },
    ],
    flashcards: [
      { id: `f-${R}-1`, front: "What does biological classification attempt to do?", back: "Arrange species in groups that reflect their relationships, and that may reflect their evolutionary origins." },
      { id: `f-${R}-2`, front: "What is a taxon?", back: "Any group in a classification hierarchy, at any rank (for example the family Felidae or the class Mammalia). Plural: taxa." },
      { id: `f-${R}-3`, front: "What is meant by a hierarchy in classification?", back: "Smaller groups are placed within larger groups, with no overlap between groups at the same rank." },
      { id: `f-${R}-4`, front: "The taxonomic hierarchy, largest to smallest.", back: "Domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species. (Do Keep Ponds Clean Or Frogs Get Sick.)" },
      { id: `f-${R}-5`, front: "Classify the human.", back: "Domain Eukarya, kingdom Animalia, phylum Chordata, class Mammalia, order Primates, family Hominidae, genus Homo, species Homo sapiens." },
      { id: `f-${R}-6`, front: "Classify the lion.", back: "Eukarya, Animalia, Chordata, Mammalia, Carnivora, Felidae, Panthera, Panthera leo." },
      { id: `f-${R}-7`, front: "Moving down the hierarchy, what happens to the number of species and their similarity?", back: "Each taxon contains fewer species, which share more features and (if the classification reflects evolution) a more recent common ancestor." },
      { id: `f-${R}-8`, front: "What does sharing a genus NOT tell you?", back: "That one species evolved from the other, or when their common ancestor lived." },
      { id: `f-${R}-9`, front: "Why does the specification say classification 'may' reflect evolutionary origins?", back: "Early classifications used visible similarities only; some traditional groups do not include all descendants of a common ancestor; classifications are revised as evidence appears." },
      { id: `f-${R}-10`, front: "Name the three domains. (supporting)", back: "Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya (all eukaryotes). Proposed by Woese and colleagues in 1990 from ribosomal RNA sequences." },
      { id: `f-${R}-11`, front: "Outline an immunological comparison of a protein between species.", back: "Protein (e.g. albumin) from species A injected into a rabbit; antiserum containing antibodies collected; mixed with serum from other species; amount of precipitate measured." },
      { id: `f-${R}-12`, front: "Interpreting immunological data.", back: "More precipitate means the protein is more similar to the reference species' protein, so the species is more closely related (more recent common ancestor)." },
      { id: `f-${R}-13`, front: "Limitations of immunological comparisons.", back: "One protein only; antibodies detect shape at certain sites, not the whole sequence; precipitate is semi-quantitative and depends on the antiserum; may differ in reciprocal tests." },
      { id: `f-${R}-14`, front: "How does genome sequencing clarify relationships?", back: "Aligned base sequences are compared; fewer differences means more closely related, because mutations accumulate in each lineage after species diverge." },
      { id: `f-${R}-15`, front: "Why is sequence data from many genes more reliable than an immunological test?", back: "Far more data; exact counts of every base difference, including silent and non-coding changes; less affected by one unusual gene." },
      { id: `f-${R}-16`, front: "Give an example of a classification changed by molecular evidence. (supporting)", back: "Great apes were once in Pongidae, separate from humans; genome data show chimpanzees are closer to humans than to orangutans, so great apes and humans are now in Hominidae." },
    ],
    questions: [
      // ---- MCQ ----
      { id: `q-${R}-m1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Biological classification attempts to arrange species into groups that:", options: ["are the same size", "reflect their relationships and may reflect their evolutionary origins", "share the same habitat", "are listed alphabetically"], answer: 1, explanation: "Specification wording." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m2`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which taxon in this list contains the most species?", options: ["Genus", "Family", "Class", "Kingdom"], answer: 3, explanation: "Higher taxa contain more species: kingdom is the largest of these." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m3`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Carnivora is a taxon that contains several families, including Felidae (cats) and Canidae (dogs). At which rank is Carnivora?", options: ["Class", "Order", "Genus", "Phylum"], answer: 1, explanation: "Order lies above family." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m4`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Two species are in the same order but different families. Which statement must be true?", options: ["They are in the same class", "They are in the same genus", "They are in different phyla", "They can interbreed"], answer: 0, explanation: "An order lies entirely within one class, phylum, kingdom and domain." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m5`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which describes a hierarchical classification?", options: ["Groups overlap, so a species can be in two families", "Smaller groups are placed within larger groups with no overlap", "All groups are the same size", "Species are arranged by size"], answer: 1, explanation: "Nested groups, no overlap." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m6`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "If a classification reflects evolutionary origins, two species in the same genus:", options: ["share a more recent common ancestor than two species in the same family but different genera", "must have evolved one from the other", "must be able to interbreed", "share no features with other genera"], answer: 0, explanation: "Lower shared taxon: more recent common ancestor." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m7`, ref: R, stmts: S(1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "A student says: 'Humans and chimpanzees are in the same family, so humans evolved from chimpanzees.' Which is the best correction?", options: ["Humans and chimpanzees are not in the same family", "Sharing a family shows they share a common ancestor; neither living species evolved from the other", "Chimpanzees evolved from humans", "Families have nothing to do with relationships"], answer: 1, explanation: "Both descend from a common ancestor." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m8`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "The human and the house sparrow are both in phylum Chordata. In which taxon are they different?", options: ["Domain", "Kingdom", "Class", "None"], answer: 2, explanation: "Mammalia v Aves." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m9`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "In an immunological comparison, why does serum from the reference species give the most precipitate?", options: ["It contains the most protein", "The antibodies were produced against its own protein, so they fit it exactly", "It is the most closely related to the rabbit", "Its serum is the most concentrated"], answer: 1, explanation: "It is the 100 % reference." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m10`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Why does a closely related species give more precipitate with an antiserum than a distantly related species?", options: ["Its protein has a more similar amino acid sequence and shape, so more antibodies bind", "It has more antibodies in its blood", "Its protein is larger", "Its blood clots more easily"], answer: 0, explanation: "Similar sequence, similar shape, more binding, more precipitate." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m11`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: `${ILL} Antiserum against the albumin of species J gave these results (% of the precipitate with J's own serum): K 23, L 71, M 48. Which list puts K, L and M in order from most to least closely related to J?`, options: ["K, M, L", "L, M, K", "M, L, K", "L, K, M"], answer: 1, explanation: "Most precipitate first: L (71), M (48), K (23)." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m12`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Which is a limitation of immunological comparisons that genome sequencing avoids?", options: ["They need living cells", "They compare only one protein, and only the shape of parts of it", "They cannot be used on mammals", "They give exact counts of differences"], answer: 1, explanation: "Sequencing compares many genes base by base." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m13`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: `${ILL} The number of base differences in a gene between four species is: F–G 14, F–H 3, F–I 22, G–H 13. Which species is most closely related to F?`, options: ["G", "H", "I", "F and G equally"], answer: 1, explanation: "Fewest differences: H (3)." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m14`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Why can comparing DNA base sequences reveal differences between species that comparing their proteins cannot?", options: ["Because the genetic code is degenerate, some base changes do not change the amino acid; and much DNA does not code for protein", "Because proteins contain no information", "Because DNA is found only in closely related species", "Because antibodies bind to DNA"], answer: 0, explanation: "Silent and non-coding differences are detected only by sequencing." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m15`, ref: R, stmts: S(3, 1), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Great apes were once placed in a family separate from humans. Genome comparisons show chimpanzees are more closely related to humans than to orangutans. Why did this lead to great apes being placed in the Hominidae with humans?", options: ["Because a family containing orangutans and chimpanzees but not humans would not reflect evolutionary relationships", "Because humans are apes in appearance", "Because chimpanzees can interbreed with humans", "Because orangutans are extinct"], answer: 0, explanation: "Classification attempts to reflect relationships and evolutionary origins." },
      { id: `q-${R}-m16`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "mcq", marks: 1, stem: "Antiserum against species A protein gives 52 % precipitate with species B and 51 % with species C. Which conclusion is most appropriate?", options: ["B is more closely related to A than C is", "The difference is too small to separate B and C from these data alone", "B and C are the same species", "A is not related to B or C"], answer: 1, explanation: "A one-point difference in a semi-quantitative test is not meaningful." },
      // ---- gap ----
      { id: `q-${R}-g1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: "Complete the hierarchy.", text: "domain, {{1}}, phylum, {{2}}, order, {{3}}, genus, species", answers: { 1: ["kingdom"], 2: ["class"], 3: ["family"] }, explanation: "Kingdom, class, family." },
      { id: `q-${R}-g2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "gap", marks: 3, stem: `The alignment shows 30 bases of the same gene in four imaginary species.<div class="figure"><div class="fig-view">${A.align()}</div></div>`, text: "Differences between Q and S: {{1}}. Between Q and R: {{2}}. Between S and R: {{3}}.", answers: { 1: [String(mx.Q.S)], 2: [String(mx.Q.R)], 3: [String(mx.S.R)] }, explanation: `Q–S ${mx.Q.S}, Q–R ${mx.Q.R}, S–R ${mx.S.R}.` },
      // ---- matching ----
      { id: `q-${R}-mt1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "match", marks: 3, stem: "Match each taxon in the classification of the lion to its rank.", pairs: [{ left: "Chordata", right: "Phylum" }, { left: "Mammalia", right: "Class" }, { left: "Carnivora", right: "Order" }, { left: "Felidae", right: "Family" }, { left: "Panthera", right: "Genus" }] },
      { id: `q-${R}-mt2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "match", marks: 2, stem: "Match each term from an immunological comparison to its meaning.", pairs: [{ left: "Antiserum", right: "Serum containing antibodies against the reference species' protein" }, { left: "Precipitate", right: "Insoluble complexes of antibody bound to protein" }, { left: "Reference species", right: "The species whose protein was injected; its serum gives 100 %" }, { left: "Large precipitate with a test species", right: "Its protein is very similar in shape to the reference protein" }] },
      // ---- sequence ----
      { id: `q-${R}-sq1`, ref: R, stmts: S(2), type: "sequence", marks: 3, stem: "Put the ranks in order from the largest taxon to the smallest.", steps: ["Domain", "Kingdom", "Phylum", "Class", "Order", "Family", "Genus", "Species"] },
      { id: `q-${R}-sq2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "sequence", marks: 2, stem: "Put the steps of an immunological comparison in order.", steps: ["Albumin from species A is injected into a rabbit", "The rabbit produces antibodies against A's albumin", "Antiserum is collected from the rabbit's blood", "The antiserum is mixed with serum from each species being compared", "The amount of precipitate in each tube is measured and compared with that for species A"] },
      // ---- labelling ----
      { id: `q-${R}-lab1`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "label", drag: true, marks: 4, stem: "The diagram shows an immunological comparison. Label the four numbered parts.", svg: A.immuno({ numbers: true }), labels: [{ n: 1, answer: "Protein (albumin) from the reference species" }, { n: 2, answer: "Antiserum containing antibodies" }, { n: 3, answer: "Serum from a species being compared" }, { n: 4, answer: "Precipitate" }], bank: ["Protein (albumin) from the reference species", "Antiserum containing antibodies", "Serum from a species being compared", "Precipitate", "DNA sample", "Red blood cells", "Fertile offspring"] },
      // ---- short answer and data ----
      { id: `q-${R}-s1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Explain what is meant by (a) a taxon and (b) a hierarchical classification. Use the domestic cat, <em>Felis catus</em>, as an example. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "(a) A taxon is a group at any rank in the classification", marks: 1 }, { point: "(a) e.g. Felidae is a taxon (family) / Felis a genus / Mammalia a class", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) Smaller groups within larger groups with no overlap, e.g. genus Felis within family Felidae within order Carnivora", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) A taxon is any group in a classification, at whatever rank: for the domestic cat, the genus Felis, the family Felidae and the class Mammalia are all taxa. (b) In a hierarchy, smaller groups are placed within larger groups and groups at the same rank do not overlap: the genus Felis lies entirely within the family Felidae, which lies within the order Carnivora, and so on up to the domain.", commonErrors: "Defining a taxon as a species only." },
      { id: `q-${R}-d1`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "short", marks: 4, stem: `Data ${ILL}. Antiserum was made against serum albumin from a species of bat, species A, and tested with serum from three other bats.\n${tbl([["A", 100], ["B", 74], ["C", 31], ["D", 88]], ["Serum from species", "Precipitate / % of that with A"])}(a) Which species is most closely related to A? (1 mark) (b) Explain how the data show this. (2 marks) (c) Give one reason why these data alone might not be enough to classify the bats. (1 mark)`, scheme: [{ point: "(a) D", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) D gives the most precipitate (88 %) of the test species", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) So its albumin is most similar in shape / amino acid sequence to A's: more antibodies bind; more recent common ancestor", marks: 1 }, { point: "(c) One of: only one protein compared; semi-quantitative / no repeats; only compares with A, not B with C; antibodies detect only some sites", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) Species D. (b) Of the three test species, D's serum produced the most precipitate, 88 % of that with A's own serum. This means more of the antibodies against A's albumin bound to D's albumin, so D's albumin is the most similar in shape, and so in amino acid sequence, to A's; D therefore shares the most recent common ancestor with A. (c) Only one protein has been compared, so the result may not represent the whole genome; sequence data from many genes would be more reliable.", commonErrors: "Choosing A; saying more precipitate means less related." },
      { id: `q-${R}-d2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: `Data ${ILL}. Scientists sequenced three genes in three species of beetle, E, F and G, and counted the base differences between each pair.\n${tbl([["Gene 1", 12, 30, 28], ["Gene 2", 25, 19, 31], ["Gene 3", 8, 27, 26], ["All three genes", 45, 76, 85]], ["", "E–F", "E–G", "F–G"])}(a) Using all three genes, which two species are most closely related? (1 mark) (b) Gene 2 suggests a different answer. Explain why scientists compare many genes rather than relying on one. (2 marks)`, scheme: [{ point: "(a) E and F (45 differences, the fewest)", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) Different genes can change at different rates / one gene can give a misleading result (e.g. gene 2 suggests E and G)", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) Using many genes / more bases gives more data, so the overall result is more reliable / less affected by one unusual gene", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) E and F, which differ at 45 bases in total, fewer than E–G (76) or F–G (85). (b) Gene 2 alone suggests that E and G are most closely related, because genes do not all change at the same rate and one gene can give a misleading picture. Comparing many genes, or whole genomes, uses far more data, so the overall pattern is more reliable and less affected by any one unusual gene.", commonErrors: "Adding the figures incorrectly; trusting gene 2 because it is different." },
      { id: `q-${R}-d3`, ref: R, stmts: S(3, 1), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Data. When the chimpanzee genome was compared with the human genome in 2005, about 1.2 % of aligned single bases differed. Whole-genome comparisons show fewer differences between humans and chimpanzees than between humans and any other living species. (a) What do these data suggest about the relationship between humans and chimpanzees? (1 mark) (b) Explain why the base sequence of DNA can be used in this way. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "(a) Chimpanzees are humans' closest living relatives / share the most recent common ancestor with humans", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) Mutations change base sequences over time, separately in each lineage after species diverge", marks: 1 }, { point: "(b) So the more recently two species shared a common ancestor, the fewer differences have accumulated / fewer differences means more closely related", marks: 1 }], model: "(a) Chimpanzees (with bonobos) are the closest living relatives of humans: they share a more recent common ancestor with us than any other living species does. (b) After two lineages separate, mutations change their DNA independently, so differences in base sequence accumulate with time. Species that shared a common ancestor more recently have had less time to accumulate differences, so fewer differences indicate a closer relationship.", commonErrors: "Saying humans evolved from chimpanzees; saying the 1.2 % shows chimpanzees are 98.8 % human." },
      { id: `q-${R}-s2`, ref: R, stmts: S(3), type: "short", marks: 3, stem: "Give three limitations of using an immunological comparison of one protein to decide how closely species are related. (3 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Only one protein / small part of the genome; different proteins may give different answers", marks: 1 }, { point: "Antibodies detect shape at certain (antigenic) sites only, not the whole sequence; silent / non-coding DNA differences undetected", marks: 1 }, { point: "Precipitate is semi-quantitative / depends on the antiserum, concentrations and conditions; small differences unreliable; reciprocal tests may disagree", marks: 1 }], model: "The test compares only one protein, a tiny part of the genome, and another protein might give a different answer. Antibodies recognise only the shape of certain sites on the protein, so differences elsewhere in the sequence, and DNA differences that do not change the protein, are not detected. The amount of precipitate is only semi-quantitative: it depends on the particular antiserum, the concentrations and the conditions, so small differences may not be meaningful.", commonErrors: "Giving ethical points about rabbits as the only limitation." },
      { id: `q-${R}-s3`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 2), type: "short", marks: 2, stem: "The lion and the tiger share a genus; the lion and the domestic cat share a family. Explain what this suggests about their relationships, and state one thing it does not show. (2 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Lion and tiger are more closely related / share a more recent common ancestor than lion and domestic cat", marks: 1 }, { point: "Does not show: that one evolved from the other / when the common ancestor lived / that they can interbreed", marks: 1 }], model: "Because the lion and tiger share a lower taxon, the genus Panthera, they are more closely related, with a more recent common ancestor, than the lion and the domestic cat, which share only the family Felidae. It does not show that either species evolved from the other, or how long ago their common ancestor lived.", commonErrors: "Saying lions and tigers are the same species." },
      // ---- extended ----
      { id: `q-${R}-e1`, ref: R, stmts: S(1, 3), type: "extended", marks: 6, stem: "Extended response. Describe how immunological comparisons and genome sequencing can be used to clarify the relationships between species, and evaluate the two methods. (6 marks)", scheme: [{ point: "Immunology: protein (e.g. albumin) of one species injected into an animal (rabbit); antiserum with antibodies collected and mixed with serum of other species", marks: 1 }, { point: "More precipitate = more similar protein = more closely related / more recent common ancestor", marks: 1 }, { point: "Sequencing: base sequences of genes / genomes aligned and compared; fewer differences = more closely related (mutations accumulate after divergence)", marks: 1 }, { point: "Evaluation of immunology: one protein; shape at antigenic sites only; semi-quantitative", marks: 1 }, { point: "Evaluation of sequencing: much more data, exact counts, detects silent / non-coding differences; but genes vary in rate / need many genes", marks: 1 }, { point: "Both test whether classification reflects evolutionary relationships; agreement strengthens conclusions; molecular data have led to revisions (e.g. great apes into Hominidae)", marks: 1 }], levels: "Level 3 (5–6): both methods described accurately with correct interpretation, and a balanced evaluation of each with a justified comparison. Level 2 (3–4): both methods described with interpretation, limited evaluation; or one method described and evaluated well. Level 1 (1–2): basic description of one method or interpretation only.", model: "In an immunological comparison, a protein such as serum albumin from one species is injected into an animal, often a rabbit, which makes antibodies against it. The antiserum is collected and mixed with serum from other species. Antibodies bind to albumin with a similar shape, forming a precipitate: the more precipitate, the more similar the protein and the more closely related the species, because they share a more recent common ancestor. In genome sequencing, the base sequences of genes or whole genomes are aligned and the differences counted; because mutations accumulate separately in each lineage after species diverge, fewer differences mean a closer relationship. Immunological tests are relatively simple, but they compare only one protein, detect only the shape of the sites where antibodies bind, and give semi-quantitative results that depend on the antiserum, so small differences are unreliable. Sequencing provides vastly more data, gives exact counts of differences and detects changes that do not alter proteins, such as silent mutations and changes in non-coding DNA; however, different genes can change at different rates, so many genes must be compared. Where the methods agree, confidence in a relationship is high. Molecular evidence of this kind has led to revisions of classification, such as placing the great apes in the family Hominidae with humans.", commonErrors: "Saying more precipitate means less related; describing sequencing without explaining why fewer differences mean a closer relationship." },
    ],
  };
})();
